# pekko-actor

The core of goPekko: actors, lifecycle, dead letters and mailboxes, redesigned
over goroutines. Source of truth: Apache Pekko v1.7.1. This module imports no
other goPekko module.


## Usage and behavior

Create a `System` with `NewSystem`, implement `Actor[T].Receive`, and pass a
factory to `Spawn` to obtain a typed `Ref[T]`. Send messages with `ref.Tell`;
request termination with `sys.Stop(h)`, `ctx.Stop(h)` or `ctx.StopSelf()`. Wait on the
channel returned by `sys.Done(ref)` to observe completion. See the
[executable examples](actor/example_test.go) and [`ExampleAsk`](actor/ask_example_test.go).

- Each actor processes messages serially from a FIFO mailbox, unbounded by default.
  Concurrent senders are ordered by mailbox admission, not call start time.
  `Tell` ignores its context; it offers no delivery acknowledgement or backpressure.
- **Bounded mailbox (opt-in; [ADR 0030](../docs/adr/0030-bounded-mailbox.md), accepted by the Owner on 2026-10-22).**
  `Spawn(owner, name, factory, actor.WithMailboxCapacity(n))` is Pekko Classic's `BoundedMailbox(n, pushTimeOut = 0)`
  (the mailbox id `bounded-capacity:n`), which Typed's `MailboxSelector.bounded(n)` also builds. When `n` messages are waiting, `Tell` does not block and does not fail:
  the message is published as a `DeadLetter` to the actor. The message being processed does not count.
  A `Terminated` delivery and a `Port` post take a slot like any message, as in Pekko (where both are messages to
  the `ActorRef`). A `Terminated` that finds none is dead-lettered and the watcher is not told (a later `Watch` of the
  dead subject delivers it); a rejected `Port.Post` returns `false` and is one `DeadLetter`. After `Unwatch`, a queued
  `Terminated` keeps its slot until it is taken and is then ignored. Stop, restart, suspend, resume and child-failure
  items are outside the bound, as Pekko's system messages are. `n < 1` returns `ErrInvalidMailboxCapacity`. This is
  **not backpressure**: the sender learns nothing. Without `WithMailboxCapacity` the capacity stays unbounded; that is
  a statement about capacity only. The death notification is a system message for **every** actor, with or without a
  capacity: the `Terminated` and the `WatchWith` message go to the tail of the mailbox when the notification is
  processed, so a message told after the death but before that processing is handled before them (ADR 0030 D8,
  amendment of ADR 0010).
  **Open and pre-existing differences** (ADR 0030 sections 3 and 4): after a dropped `Terminated`, a re-watch with
  another `WatchWith` message delivers the first one, as Pekko does (decided, ADR 0030 D4); a dropped `Terminated` is a plain `DeadLetter`, not a suppressed one; a `Terminated` still waiting when the
  watcher stops is discarded, not dead-lettered. Other Pekko variants (blocking `BoundedMailbox`,
  `NonBlockingBoundedMailbox`, bounded priority/deque/control-aware, bounded stash) are not implemented: #685, #688,
  #182. What evidence backs this contract, what ran locally, what did not (no `-race`, no JVM scenario, no remote CI)
  and which points await the Owner is in [`docs/parity/mbx-13/`](../docs/parity/mbx-13/README.md) (story #791); it
  claims no parity with Pekko.
- Optional `PreStart` runs before message processing. An error or panic in `Receive` or in `PreStart` is reported to
  the parent's decider ([Supervision (units U1 to U5)](#supervision-units-u1-to-u5)): the parent's own strategy if it
  declares one (which may `Restart` the child within a `Budget`), and `DefaultStrategy` otherwise (restart without a
  limit for an ordinary error, stop for an initialization failure). A top-level actor is a child of the `/user`
  guardian (unit U5b), so the same default applies to it. An error or panic in `PostStop` stops the actor.
- `ctx.Stop(h)` is `sys.Stop(h)` from inside a handler: `h` may be a child, a sibling, the parent or the actor itself
  (stopping oneself is allowed); an invalid, foreign or stopped handle is a no-op. It means "stop accepted, not stop
  completed": it returns before the target finished, and `Done` tells when it has.
- `Stop` returns after accepting termination. An in-flight handler completes;
  queued messages go to dead letters in FIFO order. If the stop is accepted before
  the first activation, the stop still takes the start step: `PreStart` runs once,
  then `PostStop` once, with no `Receive` ([ADR 0012](../docs/adr/0012-stop-before-first-activation.md),
  this case only). Optional `PostStop` then runs once, after every child of the actor finished (see "Recursive
  stop" below); cleanup releases the name, publishes the pending messages and closes `Done`.
  A panic in `PostStop`, or a `PostStop` that ends its goroutine with `runtime.Goexit` (what `t.FailNow` does in a
  test actor), does not strand cleanup: it is reported as `poststop-panic`, and the rest of the finalization (dead
  letters, name release, watchers, `Done`) still runs, with the `PostStop` of termination run exactly once ([ADR 0014](../docs/adr/0014-actor-children-and-hierarchical-termination.md) D6).
- **A Stop during the wait of a restart (U7, #449; PR #472).** The default `PreRestart` stops the children and calls `PostStop` on the old instance at once, without waiting for them and with no guard (`Actor.scala:625-631`); a custom `PreRestart` is user code. If a Stop arrives while the restart waits for the children, the restart is **abandoned**: no new instance, no `PostRestart`, no second `PreStart`. The termination then runs `PostStop` once more on the same (old) instance after the children finished, and `Terminated` is delivered once. With the default hooks the old instance therefore sees **two** `PostStop` calls; with a `PreRestart` that does not call it, one. Counts and order follow `migration/stage-1/supervision/h4-repro/RESULTS.md` (Pekko v1.7.1, accepted by the Owner in ADR 0017 H4/S11). Verified by the Go tests `TestSup2PostStopGuard` (W11a/b) and `TestSupStopDuringRestart` (T1-T3) and by the judge scenario S22 (concurrent mode), on Pekko and, since #173, in the Go gate: the default `PreRestart` emits the unexported diagnostics event `prerestart-default` (first statement of `defaultPreRestart`), which the Go harness maps to `pre-restart` for nodes with no `PreRestart` method, and the directed mutant `GMdefaultps` (the pre-U7 guard on the default hook's `PostStop`) must fail S22 by `G5.f`. Before U7 Go ran one `PostStop` in this situation; the double call is Pekko's and Go has it since U7. S22 is not calibrated against Pekko mutations. Go-specific and with no Pekko equivalent (`TestSupStopAfterGoexitInP1`, T4): if the `PostStop` of the default `PreRestart` ends with `runtime.Goexit` and a Stop then arrives, termination runs `PostStop` again. No parity is claimed.
- **Local deathwatch** ([ADR 0010](../docs/adr/0010-actor-api.md), "Accepted for the local watch increment").
  Inside `PreStart` and `Receive`, `ctx.Watch(h)` asks to be told when `h` terminates: the actor then gets one
  `Terminated` through the optional `OnTerminated(ctx, Terminated) error`, after the watched actor's `PostStop`.
  The death notification is a system message, as in Pekko: the actor processes it between two turns, never inside
  a handler, and the `Terminated` goes to the **tail** of the mailbox as it is then, behind every message already
  there (amendment of ADR 0010, ADR 0030 E16). `ctx.WatchWith(h, msg)` delivers `msg` to `Receive`
  instead. `ctx.Unwatch(h)` withdraws the registration and discards a `Terminated` already queued for it. Watching
  an actor that has already stopped delivers too; watching oneself does nothing. A `Terminated` with no
  `OnTerminated` stops the watcher (`ErrDeathPact`); registering again with another message, or mixing `Watch`
  and `WatchWith`, fails it with `ErrWatchConflict` (the parent's decider chooses; the default strategy restarts it, as it restarts an `IllegalStateException` in Pekko) (a repeat is a no-op only for a message comparable with `==`);
  a nil or non-actor handle fails it with `ErrInvalidHandle`; a handle of another System of the same process is accepted for `Watch`, `WatchWith` and `Unwatch` (see ADR 0010). Each registration has its own generation,
  so a notification of an earlier registration is discarded. A watcher that stops releases its links at subjects of another System on
  that System's executor, after its own stop was accepted; the release removes only that watcher's links, and `Unwatch` removes the link at
  once. The cross-System stop, `Unwatch` and termination behavior (DTH-02, #827) is covered by Go-only tests in
  [`actor/watch_cross_system_dth02_internal_test.go`](actor/watch_cross_system_dth02_internal_test.go); Pekko has no two-System spec, so
  no parity is claimed. These runtime items are not messages and are never
  dead-lettered. A death pact or an `OnTerminated` failure is a failure of the watcher reported to its parent's decider
  (the default strategy stops it on a death pact and restarts it on an `OnTerminated` error, unit U3); the same holds for a top-level
  watcher, decided by `/user`. Not here: remote watchers, `existenceConfirmed` and `ChildFailed`.
- Messages sent after stop acceptance go directly to dead letters. They may
  interleave with pending messages being published during cleanup; there is
  no global dead-letter ordering across publishers.
- Dead-letter subscribers run synchronously on the publisher goroutine and
  may run concurrently. They must be safe for concurrent calls, must not block
  and must not panic. A slow subscriber delays the publisher, including a
  `Tell` to a stopping actor. A subscriber panic propagates to the publisher.
  The hub's barrier is internal; it is not a public completion API.

The message admission paths are:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    T["Tell"] --> S{"Stop accepted?"}
    S -->|No| Q["Queue in FIFO mailbox"]
    Q --> R["Serial Receive"]
    S -->|Yes| D["Publish dead letter"]
    R --> F{"Error or panic?"}
    F -->|Yes| X["Accept stop, or report to a parent that declares a strategy"]
    F -->|No| N["Next activation step"]
```

Sources: [message admission](actor/ref.go), [activation and hooks](actor/cell.go),
[stop and cleanup](actor/stop.go), [publication hub](internal/deadletter/hub.go).

## Ask: request and reply ([ADR 0015](../docs/adr/0015-ask-and-reply.md))

`Ask` sends a request that carries its own reply address and returns the first reply:

```go
type Get struct{ ReplyTo actor.Ref[int] }

ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), time.Second)
defer cancel()
n, err := actor.Ask(ctx, counter, func(r actor.Ref[int]) Get { return Get{ReplyTo: r} })
```

The target replies with `msg.ReplyTo.Tell(ctx, 42)`. A runnable version is [`ExampleAsk`](actor/ask_example_test.go).

- **It blocks.** `Ask` blocks the calling goroutine until the first reply, the deadline or the cancellation of `ctx`.
- **A deadline is mandatory.** A live context without a deadline returns `ErrAskNoDeadline` and nothing is sent; there is
  no default timeout.
- **From `Receive` it blocks that actor.** An actor that calls `Ask` inside `Receive` processes no other message while it
  waits, and the deadline or cancellation is its only exit if the reply never comes. An actor that asks itself can end only
  by the deadline or cancellation, unless something else replies through the reply address.
  Two actors that ask each other stay blocked in the same way: each `Receive` waits for a request that only the other
  could read, and only the end of a `ctx` or a reply sent through a reply address ends the wait. A `Stop` of the asking
  actor is accepted, but its `PostStop` runs only after `Ask` returns (R3); that `Done` closes after `PostStop` follows from
  the order in `finalize` (`stop.go`), which R3 does not observe. Cancelling `ctx` cancels the wait, not the work of the
  recipient: the request, if it was sent, stays in the recipient's mailbox and is processed later, and a reply that arrives
  after the wait ended is dead-lettered. If `ctx` ends while `build` runs, the request is not sent at all.
  Evidence: [`ask_receive_test.go`](actor/ask_receive_test.go) R1, R1b, R2, R3 and `TestAskAPI_E7_AskFromReceiveBlocksTheActor`.
  Pekko's `ask` returns a `Future` without blocking the caller (`AskSupport.scala:346-347`, `internalAsk` 372-380); the
  `Future` completes with the reply, with `AskTimeoutException` at the timeout (`:725-738`), or, in Classic, fails at once when the
  recipient had already terminated (`:373-375`; Typed fails with `TimeoutException`). A self-Ask or a cycle between two Pekko actors is not blocked, but it
  completes only if the actor handles the request and replies; otherwise the `Future` fails at the timeout. This is an
  adaptation of the API ([ADR 0015](../docs/adr/0015-ask-and-reply.md) D1), not parity. A first-class helper is tracked in #675.
- **Safe use from `Receive` with today's API.** Return from `Receive` at once and let a goroutine do the `Ask`; the result
  comes back as a message. This is the hand-written shape of Classic's `ask` + `pipeTo(self)`. Runnable version:
  [`ExampleAsk_fromReceive`](actor/ask_receive_example_test.go).

  ```go
  func (a *Asker) Receive(c *actor.Context[Msg], m Msg) error {
      if m.IsReply {              // the result is handled here, inside Receive
          if m.Err == nil { a.total += m.Value }
          return nil
      }
      self, peer, timeout := c.Self(), a.peer, a.timeout // copied here: a Context is valid only during this call
      go func() {
          ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), timeout) // per request, owned by this goroutine
          defer cancel()
          v, err := actor.Ask(ctx, peer, func(r actor.Ref[int]) Req { return Req{ReplyTo: r} })
          self.Tell(context.Background(), Msg{IsReply: true, Value: v, Err: err})
      }()
      return nil
  }
  ```

  - The goroutine touches neither the `Context` nor the actor's fields; it only uses the copies. State changes only in `Receive`.
  - Create the `ctx` inside the goroutine for each request. A deadline stored in a struct is fixed when it is created: once
    it passes, every later `Ask` returns `ErrAskTimeout` at once. Deriving it from a parent `ctx` lets the application cancel
    the wait on shutdown; nothing cancels it when `Receive` returns or when the actor stops.
  - The deadline bounds the wait inside `Ask`. It does not stop the recipient and does not stop any other work the goroutine does.
  - Errors travel in the message (`Err`). If the actor has stopped, or its bounded mailbox is full, `Tell` dead-letters the reply.
  - Replies of concurrent requests arrive in no defined order; put a correlation value in the request if it matters.
  - The goroutine is not supervised by the actor. A panic in it (including one from `build`) ends the process unless the
    goroutine recovers it, and nothing limits how many goroutines concurrent requests start.
  - Evidence: `ExampleAsk_fromReceive` and `TestAskFromReceive_R4_GoroutinePatternKeepsTheActorResponsive` (it uses a
    far-away deadline held in a field to keep the test short; the per-request `ctx` above is the shape to copy).
- **Errors are mechanism failures.** `ErrAskTimeout` (`errors.Is(err, context.DeadlineExceeded)`), `ErrAskCanceled`
  (`errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)`), `ErrAskNoDeadline`, `ErrRecipientTerminated` (the target had completed its
  termination; a merely accepted `Stop` does not give it) and `ErrInvalidHandle` (a nil or typed-nil target, a `Ref` not created by `Spawn`,
  or the reply address of another request; a ref of another `System` is not rejected, cross-`System` Ask is deferred) are
  returned as the sentinels themselves. A business error travels inside the reply value `R` and the error of `Ask` stays nil: this is a
  Go convention (AK11) and differs from classic Pekko, where a `Status.Failure` reply fails the future. No parity with Pekko
  is claimed.
- **The first reply wins.** A later reply is dead-lettered. `build` is called at most once, on the caller's goroutine, and not
  at all when the target is invalid or the context has already ended or lacks a usable deadline.

The check order, the single winner among reply, timeout and cancellation, and the cleanup of the timer are covered by the
internal tests with a fake clock; the external tests in [`ask_api_test.go`](actor/ask_api_test.go) cover the public contract.

## Children, names and recursive stop ([ADR 0014](../docs/adr/0014-actor-children-and-hierarchical-termination.md), slices 2 and 3)

Implemented: identity, ownership and names. `Spawn(owner, name, factory)` takes a `Spawner`, which is a `*System`
(a top-level actor, a child of the `/user` guardian) or the `*Context[T]` of a running actor (a child of that actor). The child's
protocol is inferred from its factory and may differ from the parent's. `ctx.Parent()`, `ctx.Child(name)` and
`ctx.Children()` give identities (`Handle`) only: such a handle has no `Tell`, and `Watch`, `Unwatch` and
`System.Stop` and `ctx.Stop` accept it because they resolve a handle by its ID alone.

```go
func (b boss) Receive(ctx *actor.Context[startMsg], _ startMsg) error {
	child, err := actor.Spawn(ctx, "greeter", func() actor.Actor[string] { return greeter(b) })
	if err != nil {
		return err // ErrInvalidName, ErrNameTaken or ErrParentStopping
	}
	child.Tell(context.Background(), "hello")
	return nil
}
```

See the [executable example](actor/hierarchy_test.go).

- `Spawn` returns the `Ref` of a **committed** child and never calls the factory: the child's first activation runs it,
  through the executor, before `PreStart`. A nil actor, a panic or a `runtime.Goexit` of the factory is an
  `*InitializationError` decided by the parent (default Stop; a top-level child is decided by `/user`, U5b), reported with the event `factory-failed`; it is not an error of `Spawn`. `Spawn` has exactly three
  synchronous errors: `ErrInvalidName`, `ErrNameTaken` and `ErrParentStopping`. `ErrNilActor`, `ErrFactoryPanic` and
  `FactoryPanicValue` stay exported as the **cause** a decider or an event receives. With an inline executor the first
  activation, and so the factory, can run on the caller's goroutine inside `Spawn`, and the child may already have
  terminated when `Spawn` returns; the default executor never runs it inline.
- A name belongs to its owner: the same name under two parents is allowed, under one parent it is `ErrNameTaken`
  while a live child, a stopping child or a child still initializing holds it. The name is released when the child
  terminates, also after a factory failure. A recreated name is a new ID; the old `Ref`
  keeps targeting the old actor and dead-letters.
- Child names are validated before anything is reserved, with Pekko's path element rules (ASCII letters and digits,
  ``-_.*$+:@&=,!~';`` or `%XX` with two hex digits; not empty, not starting with `$`): `ErrInvalidName`, nothing is
  committed and no ID or event exists. Nothing is trimmed or decoded (`%41` is accepted as written).
  Validation comes before the stop gate: an invalid name through a stopping parent is `ErrInvalidName`, not
  `ErrParentStopping`.
  **Top-level names are validated like child names (U5c, decision D7)**: `""`, `$x`, spaces, `/` and `%zz` return `ErrInvalidName` before any reservation. This is a breaking change of ADR 0014's former D4.
- **Anonymous children (IDN-01, #765, inventory `ACTINV-002`).** `SpawnAnonymous(owner, factory, opts...)` is Pekko's
  `actorOf(props)` (`Children.scala` `randomName`, `Helpers.base64`): the owner names the child `$` plus its own counter
  in Pekko's base-64 alphabet (`$a`, `$b`, ..., `$~`, `$ab`, ...). The counter is per owner (a `*System` counts for
  `/user`), atomic, starts at 0 and never decreases, so concurrent calls get distinct names and the name of a stopped
  child is never generated again. A generated name cannot collide with an explicit one: `Spawn` rejects any name
  starting with `$`. It returns the errors of `Spawn` except `ErrInvalidName` and `ErrNameTaken`; a spawn refused after
  its options were accepted (for example `ErrParentStopping`) still consumes a counter value, as in Pekko; an invalid
  `WithMailboxCapacity` is refused first and consumes none (a difference: Pekko draws the name before validating
  props). `Spawn` with `""` stays `ErrInvalidName`; explicit-name validation and duplicate rejection are unchanged.
  Evidence: `anonymous_name_internal_test.go`. Not claimed: the other sibling stories or full actor parity. There is no
  public accessor for the name of a `Ref`, so applications find the child through `Context.Child`/`Children`.
- Identity is the actor ID. A logical path is at most metadata and is not unique (names are scoped by owner, and no name contains `/`);
  there is no way to look an actor up by path yet. The contract for local `Address` and `ActorPath` values (diagnostic only,
  never identity) is in [ADR 0031](../docs/adr/0031-local-address-and-actor-path-values.md), accepted by the Owner.
  **Implemented so far:**
  - the local `Address` value (IDN-13, #1007): `NewLocalAddress(protocol, system)`, `Protocol`, `System`, `HasLocalScope`,
    `String` (`protocol://system`), `Compare` (by UTF-16 code unit, as Pekko) and `==`, and the constant `LocalProtocol`
    (`"pekko"`, which names the printed scheme only and implies no transport, serialization or remote compatibility); see
    [`address_test.go`](actor/address_test.go). An empty protocol or system name is `ErrInvalidAddress`; the zero `Address`
    is "no address" and prints `""`. Host and port are reserved and not exposed.
  - the local `ActorPath` value (IDN-14, #1008): `NewRootPath(address)` (the root is fixed as `/`), `Child(name)` for one
    element, `Address`, `Name`, `Elements`, `IsRoot`, `String` (`pekko://sys/user/a`) and `==` as the path equality; see
    [`actor_path_test.go`](actor/actor_path_test.go). An element must be non-empty and use Pekko's path-element characters,
    **a leading `$` included**, so names from `SpawnAnonymous` fit; anything else, `/` and `#` included, is `ErrInvalidPath`
    (stricter than Pekko's `ChildActorPath`). `Spawn` is unchanged: a name you supply still cannot start with `$`. Nothing is escaped or decoded, and the zero `ActorPath` is "no path".
  - the uid of a path (IDN, #1323 part a; Owner decision of 2026-11-04, ADR 0031 amendment (b)): `ActorPath` carries the uid of the
    incarnation it names, a signed 32-bit integer; `UID()` reads it and `WithUID(uid)` sets it. **Equality has two forms.** Go's
    `==` compares the uid too (a difference of Go form, the same precedent as `time.Time`); Pekko's equality, address and
    elements without the uid, is `Equal`. `String()` ignores the uid, as Pekko's `toString` does. Use `WithUID(0)` for a map key.
    Without a uid the value is 0 and means "any incarnation". `Parent` and `Child` return the path without a uid; the root
    guardian has none. See [`actor_path_uid_test.go`](actor/actor_path_uid_test.go), [`uid_internal_test.go`](actor/uid_internal_test.go).
  - the uid of a spawned actor: each parent incarnation has its own counter that starts at a random 32-bit value (`crypto/rand`);
    the uid of each child is a bijection of start+k on the 32-bit integers, skipping 0. It is **strictly distinct among the
    children of one parent incarnation**; between incarnations of the parent it is probabilistic, like Pekko's random draw. On
    exhaustion the counter wraps: no panic and no spawn failure. The uid is an attribute of the incarnation, derived and not a
    second identity: identity stays the `ID` (ADR 0024). `PathOf(h)` returns the path with the uid of its incarnation. The
    Owner decision, verbatim, is in ADR 0031 (note after amendment (b)).
  - path navigation (IDN-04, #852): `Parent()` (the root is its own parent; the zero path stays zero) and
    `Descendant(names...)` (empty names are skipped; any other name goes through the same check as `Child`, and a bad name
    fails the whole call with `ErrInvalidPath`; on the zero path it always returns the zero path and `ErrInvalidPath`, on a
    valid path no names or only empty names return the receiver); see [`actor_path_test.go`](actor/actor_path_test.go).
    Both are pure value operations: they take no `System` and look up no actor. Evidence: Go not run on a JVM; Pekko rows
    executed in a disposable copy (see PR #1292); no parity claim. **Open divergence, not accepted (#1328):** Pekko's `/`
    splits `name#uid` (executed row 69 of PR #1292 for `fromString`), accepts an empty name and any character but `/` and `#`;
    here `Descendant` and `Child` reject them (`actor_path_divergence_test.go` pins it). ADR 0031 P-7 is withdrawn.
  - the path of a reference (IDN-05, #853): the free function `PathOf(h Handle) (ActorPath, bool)` reports the logical path an
    actor was spawned at (`pekko://sys/user/a`, nested `pekko://sys/user/a/b`, anonymous `pekko://sys/user/$a`, the three
    guardians included). The path is fixed when the actor is committed and kept by a restart and after termination. A `Ref`
    reports a logical path; identity is the `ID`: path equality is not identity, so two incarnations that used one path at
    different times report paths that are `Equal` (and differ in `UID`) and distinct `ID`s. `PathOf` adds no method to `Ref[T]` or `Handle`. It answers
    `(zero, false)` for a nil or typed-nil handle, a handle this package did not issue (a user type), a hand-built cell,
    and the handles built from an `ID` alone: `Terminated.Who()` and the stale failed child a decider receives are
    deferred, not an error. A ref of another System reports its own path with that System's `Address`.
    Address equality is not proof that two handles belong to the same System instance, because `Config.Name` is neither
    validated nor unique (two Systems may share a name). See [`ref_path_internal_test.go`](actor/ref_path_internal_test.go). Evidence: Go not run
    on a JVM; Pekko rows executed in a disposable copy (see PR K, `migration/stage-1/actor-853-ref-path/pekko-repro`); no
    parity claim. The Classic rows behind N1-N4, P5, P7 and P8 are rows 1-2, 4-12, 14-27 and 28-37 of
    `migration/stage-1/actor-853-ref-path/pekko-repro/observed.txt`; they supplement the tests and replace none. A handle's
    public identity is its `ID()`; the unexported `idHandle` struct also carries the path, which does not distinguish two
    handles of one cell (one residual edge for hand-built handles, in the `idHandle` comment). Not delivered here: parsing a path from text, `Root`, resolving a reference from a path or a string, and
    parsing and resolving an exact-reference string (#812, #1089, #893, #1092); the path of an ask endpoint (`(zero, false)`).

