- **System name and local address** (IDN-15, #1009, [ADR 0031](../docs/adr/0031-local-address-and-actor-path-values.md) O3a-O3c).
  `Config.Name` names the `System`; an empty name is `"default"`, so `NewSystem(Config{})` is unchanged and still valid.
  `System.Name()` returns the effective name and `System.Address()` returns the local `Address` (`LocalProtocol` plus that name,
  for example `pekko://orders`). Both are fixed at creation, copied from `Config` (later writes to the caller's `Config` have no
  effect) and the same for the whole life of the `System`. **Limitation:** the name is used exactly as given. It is not trimmed,
  rewritten or validated, so a name that Pekko rejects (`^[a-zA-Z0-9][a-zA-Z0-9-_]*$`, `ActorSystem.scala:840-843`), such as
  `"a/b"`, is accepted and printed verbatim; an invalid name is never turned into `"default"`. Rejecting it needs a contract
  decision (`NewSystem` has no error return) and is not part of this cut. Evidence:
  [`system_name_api_test.go`](actor/system_name_api_test.go), from reading the Pekko source only; no JVM run.
- **Injectable clock** (TIM-11, #1129). `Config.Clock` takes a `Clock` (`Now() time.Time` and `AfterFunc(d, f) Timer`, with
  `Timer.Stop() bool`). `StartTime`, `Uptime` and `Ask` read it: `Ask` judges the context deadline with its `Now`, arms the
  remaining time with its `AfterFunc` and calls `Timer.Stop` once after the Ask settles, ignoring the result, so with a manual
  clock `ErrAskTimeout` is returned only when the clock fires the callback. A nil `Clock` is the wall clock and `NewSystem` keeps
  its signature. **Not implemented:** a manual clock or a wall-clock constructor is not exported (a test writes its own double,
  as [`clock_api_test.go`](actor/clock_api_test.go) does); the only timers armed on it are Ask's and the scheduler's (next item); the
  restart window of a `Strategy` does not use this clock; a `Clock` whose `AfterFunc` returns a nil `Timer` is a contract violation
  and is not defended. No Pekko parity is claimed (a Go seam has no oracle; ADR 0033 D13 evidence is pending Owner approval).
- **Scheduler, first cut** (TIM-02, #821, slice 1; contract: [ADR 0039](../docs/adr/0039-scheduler-lifecycle-and-cancellation.md),
  **Accepted**). `System.Scheduler().ScheduleOnce(delay, f)` runs `f` once on the System's executor after `delay` on the System's
  `Clock`, never early, and returns a `Cancellable` (`Cancel`, `IsCancelled`). `Cancel` is true only if it prevented the run; after
  the run, or while `f` runs, it is false and `IsCancelled` stays false. A `delay` at or below zero is not an error: `f` goes to the
  executor at once and the handle is never cancellable. A panic in `f` is recovered and written once to `Config.Logger` (nothing
  without one). A nil `f` returns `ErrNilCallback`. An actor reaches it as `ctx.System().Scheduler()` (`Context.System`, described below, was added by
  #1182 slice B; this item adds no export to `Context`). Evidence: the zero delay and the `Cancel` values follow the oracle
  [`scheduler-lifecycle-2026-11-10`](../docs/parity/oracles/scheduler-lifecycle-2026-11-10.md) (SC1, SC3o); the executor hop, the
  panic report and `ErrNilCallback` are Go-only and pending approval; tests:
  [`scheduler_internal_test.go`](actor/scheduler_internal_test.go).
  **Close on System termination** (#1131, implementation integrated by #1553): the root guardian closes the scheduler after actors' `PostStop`
  and before `WhenTerminated` closes. Pending not-yet-due tasks are dropped, not run; `IsCancelled` stays false unless the caller cancelled them.
  New schedules after close return `ErrSchedulerClosed` and arm nothing. Close is idempotent; a positive-delay schedule re-checks close after registration
  and rejects only if cancellation wins. A callback already handed to the executor cannot be recalled. `Terminate` starts an asynchronous shutdown;
  calling it does not itself mean the scheduler has closed. Internal tests: `TestSchedulerClose`, `TestSchedulerCloseRace`, `TestSchedulerTerminateCloses`,
  `TestSchedulerCloseNoPanic` and `TestSchedulerP2Recheck` (internal package). Public acceptance evidence (#1559,
  [`scheduler_close_test.go`](actor/scheduler_close_test.go)): AC-R1-1 rejects ScheduleOnce with any delay after `WhenTerminated` and arms no new timer;
  AC-R2-1 drops a pending task scheduled before `Terminate`, the clock spy records zero fires, and `IsCancelled` stays false;
  AC-R3-1 cancels a pre-existing timer and admits `ScheduleOnce` during `PostStop`, then rejects after `WhenTerminated`.
  The close-after-PostStop ordering is also visible in [`stop.go`](actor/stop.go).
  The Go judge campaign is stale after #1559 added a test under pekko-actor and changed the core inputs; renewal is a separate delivery.
  **Not implemented:** `ScheduleTellOnce` (slice 2), fixed delay and fixed rate (#822, #823), keyed timers (#860).
  Tick rounding is an accepted PORT GAP. No Pekko parity of the module is claimed.

